<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/expert/astrobiology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Astrobiology asks the biggest question with the least data: <strong>is life a cosmic
    accident or a cosmic habit?</strong> One confirmed example exists — Earth — which is not
    enough to know. The field works from both ends: astronomy finds the habitable real estate,
    and biology finds life's stubbornness at Earth's own extremes, then points the telescopes
    where the two lists overlap.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the search, organised: definition, real estate, candidates, signatures" widget="equationStep" config={astroCfg} />

  <h2>What Earth's extremophiles changed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Life is tougher than assumed:</strong> microbes thrive in acid, radiation, pressure and darkness — boiling springs, Antarctic ice, rock kilometres down. The "habitable" envelope widened enormously after the 1970s deep-sea vent discoveries.</li>
    <li><strong>Panspermia remains possible but unproven:</strong> meteorites carry amino acids (Murchison, 1969) and organics are everywhere in space. Building blocks are common; the jump to self-replication is the missing chapter.</li>
    <li><strong>The Fermi paradox:</strong> billions of years, billions of stars — where is everybody? Explanations span "life is rare", "intelligence is rare", "they're quiet", and "we're early". Every answer is currently speculation; that's what makes the question scientific once instruments exist.</li>
    <li><strong>The honest current answer:</strong> zero confirmed extraterrestrial life, but a rapidly shrinking search space — Mars sample return, Europa Clipper's ocean flybys, and exoplanet atmospheric spectroscopy are all underway. This is a field where the decisive data may arrive within decades.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NASA Astrobiology Strategy (2015);Catling & Kasting, Atmospheric Evolution on Inhabited Worlds; Ward & Brownlee, Rare Earth (the counterpoint).</p>
